---
/**
 * What the package weighs, said in the two ways that are both true.
 *
 * The numbers come from `scripts/collect-package-size.mjs`, which runs a real
 * bundle and a real `npm pack`, and CI fails when the committed file has
 * drifted. Nothing here is typed in.
 *
 * The section deliberately gives the install size as well as the bundled one.
 * Quoting ninety kilobytes alone would be true and would still feel like a
 * trick to somebody who then looks in `node_modules` and finds eight megabytes.
 * Saying both, and saying what the difference is made of, costs one line and is
 * the reason to believe the first number.
 *
 * There is no budget meter here any more, and its absence is deliberate. A
 * percentage-of-budget reading tells a reader how close the authors are to a
 * line the authors drew themselves, which is a fact about this project's
 * housekeeping rather than about the package. What a reader wants to know is
 * what the kilobytes bought, so that is what this says instead.
 */
import sizes from "../data/packageSize.json";
import stats from "../data/testStats.json";

const kb = (bytes: number) => `${Math.round(bytes / 1000)} kB`;
const mb = (bytes: number) => `${(bytes / 1_000_000).toFixed(1)} MB`;

/**
 * The gap between importing one name and importing the whole entry.
 *
 * Taken as a magnitude, because it can fall either way: the two are within a
 * few dozen bytes and which one lands higher depends on how the bundler wraps
 * a named import. Either way the point being made is that there is no
 * meaningful difference, and rendering "-60 bytes" would just look like a bug.
 */
const importGap = Math.abs(sizes.importEverythingGzip - sizes.importOneGzip);
---

<div class="band size-band not-content">
  <div class="band__inner">
    <span class="band__eyebrow">Size</span>

    <p class="size-band__figure">
      <span class="size-band__number">{kb(sizes.importOneGzip)}</span>
      <span class="size-band__unit">minified and gzipped</span>
    </p>

    <p class="size-band__lede">
      That is what reaches your users when you import the engine, measured by
      bundling it for real on every commit. It is a lot for a library, and this
      is not a library: it is a language and its runtime.
    </p>

    <p class="size-band__body">
      A lexer, a Pratt parser, a bytecode compiler and a virtual machine. Units
      with dimensional analysis, currencies, dates, times and timezones.
      Matrices, statistics and finance. A computer-algebra system that factors,
      differentiates, integrates and solves over exact rational and complex
      arithmetic. That is {stats.builtinPackages} built-in packages, all of it in
      the figure above, none of it fetched at runtime.
    </p>

    <dl class="size-band__grid">
      <div>
        <dt>{stats.runtimeDependencies}</dt>
        <dd>
          runtime dependencies, so the number above is close to what your
          lockfile grows by. The weight is the engine, not somebody else's code
          arriving behind it.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>{mb(sizes.tarballBytes)}</dt>
        <dd>downloaded from npm, across {sizes.fileCount} files.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>{mb(sizes.unpackedBytes)}</dt>
        <dd>
          on disk once installed, nearly all of it TypeScript declarations and
          the CommonJS build. None of it reaches a browser.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <p class="size-band__note">
      One thing worth being straight about: importing a single name costs
      {importGap === 0 ? " exactly the same as " : ` within ${importGap} bytes of `}
      importing everything, because every subpath reaches the engine core. The {stats.subpathExports} subpath
      exports exist so you can import from where a thing lives and so a bundler can
      resolve types correctly, not because they let you take a smaller slice. If
      that changes, this paragraph changes with it.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
